1. C# & .NET

1.1 Value types vs reference types

Short: Value types (int, struct, enum, bool) hold their data directly and are copied on assignment. Reference types (class, interface, delegate, string, arrays) hold a reference to an object on the managed heap; assignment copies the reference, not the object.

Depth:

	•	Value types usually live on the stack or inline inside their container (a struct field in a class lives on the heap with that class). "Value types live on the stack" is an oversimplification, so avoid saying it flatly.
	•	Boxing/unboxing: converting a value type to object/interface allocates on the heap. It is costly in hot paths (e.g. non-generic ArrayList). Generics avoid it.
	•	string is a reference type but immutable and has value-like semantics. Use StringBuilder for heavy concatenation.
	•	Use a struct when it is small (~16 bytes), immutable, and short-lived (readonly struct, Span<T>, record struct). Otherwise use a class.
	•	record (reference) gives value equality; record struct gives value type plus value equality.

struct P { public int X; }
var a = new P { X = 1 }; var b = a; b.X = 2;   // a.X still 1
class C { public int X; }
var c1 = new C { X = 1 }; var c2 = c1; c2.X = 2; // c1.X is 2

1.2 How do async and await work?

Short: async methods are rewritten by the compiler into a state machine. At an await on an incomplete task, the method returns an incomplete Task to the caller, freeing the thread. When the awaited operation completes, a continuation resumes the state machine. No thread is blocked while waiting for I/O.

Depth:

	•	Async is about not blocking threads during I/O waits (scalability), not about creating threads. For CPU-bound work, use Task.Run (or Parallel).
	•	SynchronizationContext: in UI/legacy ASP.NET the continuation is marshalled back to the original context. ASP.NET Core has no SynchronizationContext, so deadlocks from .Result are rare but still wasteful (thread-pool starvation). Library code should use ConfigureAwait(false).
	•	Never .Result / .Wait() / .GetAwaiter().GetResult() on async code (sync-over-async). Go async all the way.
	•	Avoid async void (exceptions can't be observed) except for event handlers. Return Task/ValueTask.
	•	Pass CancellationToken through all layers (HttpContext.RequestAborted).
	•	Run independent calls concurrently: await Task.WhenAll(t1, t2).
	•	Exceptions: awaiting rethrows the first exception; Task.WhenAll aggregates (inspect task.Exception).
	•	ValueTask for hot paths that frequently complete synchronously. Don't await a ValueTask twice.

1.3 Task vs Thread vs Task.Run()

	Thread	Task	Task.Run
What	OS-level thread you manage	Abstraction of an asynchronous operation (may not use a thread at all)	Queues a delegate to the thread pool and returns a Task
Cost	~1 MB stack, expensive to create	Cheap; uses pool threads	Cheap
Result/exception/cancel	Manual	Built in (Task<T>, AggregateException, CancellationToken, continuations)	Same

Depth:

	•	A Task represents work or a promise of completion. I/O tasks use completion ports and hold no thread.
	•	Use new Thread rarely: long-lived dedicated threads, custom priority/apartment state. Even then prefer TaskCreationOptions.LongRunning or BackgroundService.
	•	Task.Run in ASP.NET Core to "make it async" is an anti-pattern: it just moves work to another pool thread and adds overhead. It is valid for offloading CPU-bound work from a UI thread.
	•	Task.Factory.StartNew has gotchas (default scheduler, no unwrap for async lambdas). Prefer Task.Run.

1.4 Delegates vs events

Short: A delegate is a type-safe function pointer (a reference to one or more methods). An event is a delegate field with restricted access: outside the declaring class, subscribers can only += / -=, and cannot invoke it or reassign it.

Depth:

	•	Built-ins: Action, Func<>, Predicate<T>. Multicast delegates call all handlers in order.
	•	Events enforce encapsulation (publisher-subscriber). Convention: EventHandler<TEventArgs>, raise with Changed?.Invoke(this, args).
	•	Memory leak risk: a long-lived publisher keeps subscribers alive. Always unsubscribe (or use weak events).
	•	Delegates are also the basis of LINQ lambdas, callbacks, and middleware (RequestDelegate).

public class Order {
    public event EventHandler<decimal>? Paid;
    public void Pay(decimal amt) => Paid?.Invoke(this, amt);
}

1.5 IEnumerable vs IQueryable

	IEnumerable<T>	IQueryable<T>
Namespace	System.Collections.Generic	System.Linq
Executes	In memory (LINQ to Objects), Func<> delegates	Provider translates expression trees (Expression<Func<>>) into SQL
Filtering	After data is loaded	Pushed to the database
Use for	In-memory collections	EF Core / remote data sources

Depth:

	•	db.Users.Where(...) on IQueryable builds SQL: WHERE. Casting to IEnumerable early (or .ToList()) pulls the whole table, then filters in memory. A classic performance bug.
	•	Deferred execution: the query runs at enumeration (ToList, foreach, Count, First).
	•	Don't return IQueryable from repositories across layers carelessly: leaks persistence concerns and defers execution beyond the DbContext lifetime.

1.6 How does Garbage Collection work?

Short: .NET uses a generational, tracing, compacting GC. It finds objects unreachable from roots (stack, statics, registers, handles), frees them, and compacts memory.

Depth:

	•	Generations: Gen0 (new, short-lived, collected most often), Gen1 (buffer), Gen2 (long-lived). Most objects die young, so Gen0 collections are cheap. LOH (≥85,000 bytes) is collected with Gen2 and is not compacted by default; large arrays and strings fragment it. Use ArrayPool<T> to reduce churn.
	•	Modes: Workstation vs Server GC (per-core heaps, higher throughput; ASP.NET Core default is Server on multi-core), concurrent/background GC for lower pauses.
	•	Finalizers (~Class) delay reclamation (object survives one extra GC via the finalization queue). Prefer IDisposable + using/await using; use SafeHandle for native resources; implement the Dispose pattern only when owning unmanaged resources.
	•	Leaks in managed code: event handlers not unsubscribed, statics/caches that grow, Timers, captured closures, HttpClient created per request (socket exhaustion; use IHttpClientFactory).
	•	Diagnostics: dotnet-counters, dotnet-gcdump, dotnet-dump, PerfView, Visual Studio memory profiler, and watching % Time in GC and Gen2 size.

1.7 SOLID principles

	•	S: Single Responsibility: a class has one reason to change. (Split OrderService into pricing, persistence, notification.)
	•	O: Open/Closed: open for extension, closed for modification. (Add a new IDiscountStrategy instead of editing an if/else chain.)
	•	L: Liskov Substitution: subtypes must be usable wherever the base is expected without breaking behaviour. (Classic violation: Square : Rectangle, or an override that throws NotSupportedException.)
	•	I: Interface Segregation: many small, client-specific interfaces beat one fat one. (IReader/IWriter vs IRepository with 20 methods.)
	•	D: Dependency Inversion: depend on abstractions, not concretions; high-level modules don't depend on low-level ones. (Constructor-inject IEmailSender; the DI container wires the implementation.)

Senior angle: SOLID is a set of heuristics, not laws. Over-applying causes interface explosion. I apply it where change is likely and keep simple things simple (YAGNI/KISS).

1.8 Transient vs Scoped vs Singleton

Lifetime	Instance	Typical use
Transient	New every time it's requested	Lightweight, stateless services
Scoped	One per scope (one per HTTP request)	DbContext, unit-of-work, per-request state
Singleton	One for the app lifetime	Config, caches, HttpClient factory, stateless shared services

Depth:

	•	Captive dependency: a singleton must not depend on a scoped service (it would capture it forever). ValidateScopes flags this in Development. Fix: inject IServiceScopeFactory and create a scope, or change the lifetime.
	•	Singletons must be thread-safe.
	•	In BackgroundService (a singleton) create scopes manually to use a DbContext.
	•	DbContext is not thread-safe: never share across parallel tasks (use IDbContextFactory<T> for that).
	•	Keyed services (.NET 8+) let you register multiple implementations of one interface.

2. ASP.NET Core Web API

2.9 The middleware pipeline

Short: Each request flows through an ordered chain of middleware. Each component can do work before calling next(), then do work after the response comes back. It can also short-circuit.

Depth:

	•	Configured in Program.cs via app.Use*, Map, Run. Order matters. A typical order:
ExceptionHandler → HSTS → HttpsRedirection → StaticFiles → Routing → CORS → Authentication → Authorization → RateLimiter/ResponseCaching → Endpoints.
	•	Authentication must come before Authorization; CORS must come before auth for preflight to work; Exception handling first so it catches everything after it.
	•	Custom middleware:

app.Use(async (ctx, next) => {
    var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();
    await next();                       // downstream
    logger.LogInformation("{Path} took {Ms}ms", ctx.Request.Path, sw.ElapsedMilliseconds);
});

	•	Or a class with InvokeAsync(HttpContext, RequestDelegate) (conventional) / IMiddleware (factory-activated, supports scoped deps).

2.10 Authentication vs authorization; how does JWT work?

	•	Authentication (AuthN): who are you? Verifies identity (password, token, OIDC).
	•	Authorization (AuthZ): what are you allowed to do? Policies, roles, claims.

JWT: a signed token with three Base64URL parts, header.payload.signature.

	1.	Client logs in with credentials; the server validates them and issues a JWT containing claims (sub, exp, iss, aud, roles).
	2.	Client sends it in Authorization: Bearer <token>.
	3.	The API validates the signature (HMAC secret or RSA/ECDSA public key), issuer, audience, expiry (with small clock skew), then builds the ClaimsPrincipal.

Depth:

	•	JWTs are signed, not encrypted: don't put secrets in them.
	•	Short-lived access token (5–15 min) + rotating refresh token (stored HttpOnly/Secure cookie or server-side, revocable). Stateless access tokens can't be revoked before expiry, so keep them short or maintain a deny-list.
	•	Prefer asymmetric signing (RS256/ES256) when multiple services validate tokens, or delegate to an IdP (Entra ID, Auth0, IdentityServer/Duende, Keycloak) via OIDC/OAuth 2.0 (Authorization Code + PKCE for SPAs).
	•	Storage in Angular: in-memory + HttpOnly refresh cookie beats localStorage (XSS exposure).
	•	Authorization in ASP.NET Core: [Authorize(Roles="Admin")], policy-based (RequireClaim, custom IAuthorizationHandler), resource-based authorization.

builder.Services.AddAuthentication(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
  .AddJwtBearer(o => o.TokenValidationParameters = new() {
      ValidateIssuer = true, ValidateAudience = true, ValidateLifetime = true,
      ValidateIssuerSigningKey = true, ClockSkew = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(1),
      ValidIssuer = cfg["Jwt:Issuer"], ValidAudience = cfg["Jwt:Audience"],
      IssuerSigningKey = new SymmetricSecurityKey(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(cfg["Jwt:Key"]!))
  });
builder.Services.AddAuthorization(o => o.AddPolicy("CanApprove", p => p.RequireClaim("scope", "invoice.approve")));

2.11 How do you handle global exceptions?

Short: Centralise it so controllers don't need try/catch. Use the built-in exception-handling middleware with IExceptionHandler (.NET 8+) returning RFC 7807 ProblemDetails.

public class GlobalExceptionHandler(ILogger<GlobalExceptionHandler> log) : IExceptionHandler {
    public async ValueTask<bool> TryHandleAsync(HttpContext ctx, Exception ex, CancellationToken ct) {
        log.LogError(ex, "Unhandled: {TraceId}", ctx.TraceIdentifier);
        var (status, title) = ex switch {
            ValidationException   => (400, "Validation failed"),
            NotFoundException     => (404, "Not found"),
            UnauthorizedAccessException => (403, "Forbidden"),
            _                     => (500, "Server error")
        };
        ctx.Response.StatusCode = status;
        await ctx.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(new ProblemDetails {
            Status = status, Title = title, Extensions = { ["traceId"] = ctx.TraceIdentifier } }, ct);
        return true;
    }
}
// Program.cs
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<GlobalExceptionHandler>();
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();
app.UseExceptionHandler();

Depth: Map domain exceptions to status codes; never leak stack traces in production; log with correlation/trace ID (Serilog + OpenTelemetry); exception filters only cover MVC, while middleware covers the whole pipeline. For expected failures consider the Result pattern instead of exceptions (exceptions are slow and a poor fit for control flow).

2.12 Middleware vs filters

	Middleware	Filters (MVC/Minimal API)
Scope	Whole pipeline, every request (static files, health, etc.)	Only requests that reach MVC/endpoint
Context	HttpContext only	Rich: model binding, action args, ActionResult, ModelState
Use for	Cross-cutting infra: logging, CORS, auth, exception handling, compression	Action-specific logic: validation, caching of results, auditing, authorization on actions

Filter types and order: Authorization → Resource → Action → Exception → Result. Minimal APIs have endpoint filters (AddEndpointFilter). Rule of thumb: if it needs MVC context, use a filter; otherwise middleware.

2.13 API versioning and validation

Versioning (Asp.Versioning.Http/Mvc):

	•	Strategies: URL segment (/api/v1/orders, most explicit and cache-friendly), query string (?api-version=1.0), header (X-Api-Version), media type.
	•	Mark versions [ApiVersion("1.0")], [ApiVersion("2.0")], deprecate with Deprecated = true, report supported versions in headers. Integrate with Swagger (one doc per version). Avoid breaking changes: add fields rather than change/remove; version only when you must.

Validation:

	•	[ApiController] auto-returns 400 ProblemDetails for invalid ModelState.
	•	Data annotations for simple rules; FluentValidation for complex/business rules (validators per request DTO, registered via DI, run in an endpoint filter or MediatR pipeline behaviour).
	•	Validate at the edge (DTO shape), enforce invariants in the domain, and enforce constraints in the DB. Never trust the client or the Angular form validation alone.

2.14 How do you secure REST APIs?

Checklist:

	•	Transport: HTTPS everywhere, HSTS, TLS 1.2+.
	•	AuthN/AuthZ: OAuth2/OIDC + JWT bearer; least-privilege policies; check resource ownership (prevent IDOR/BOLA).
	•	Input handling: validate/sanitise; parameterised queries (EF/Dapper params) against SQLi; limit payload size; model-binding over-posting protection (use DTOs, not entities).
	•	CORS: explicit origins, never * with credentials.
	•	Rate limiting/throttling: built-in AddRateLimiter (fixed/sliding window, token bucket) to blunt brute force and DoS; WAF/API gateway in front.
	•	Secrets: Key Vault / user-secrets / env, never in source; rotate keys.
	•	Headers/hardening: no server version leakage, security headers, antiforgery for cookie-based auth.
	•	Data protection: encrypt sensitive data at rest and in transit; don't log PII/tokens.
	•	Observability: audit logs, alerting, dependency scanning (OWASP Top 10 / API Security Top 10), pen-tests.
	•	Errors: generic messages to clients, details in logs.

2.15 In-memory caching vs Redis

	IMemoryCache	Redis (IDistributedCache)
Location	Inside the app process	External shared cache server
Speed	Fastest (no network, no serialisation)	Network hop + serialisation
Multi-instance	Each instance has its own copy (inconsistent)	Shared by all instances, consistent
Survives restart	No	Yes (with persistence) / independent of app lifecycle
Capacity	Limited by app memory	Scales independently
Extras	Eviction/size limits	Pub/sub, TTL, atomic ops, locks, data structures

Use in-memory for small, rarely-changing, per-node-tolerant data (reference data, config). Use Redis for scaled-out apps: sessions, shared caches, rate-limit counters, distributed locks, SignalR backplane. A hybrid (L1 + L2) approach (HybridCache, .NET 9) gives speed plus consistency and stampede protection.
Other points: define TTL (absolute/sliding), cache-aside pattern, invalidation strategy, stampede protection (single-flight/lock), serialisation cost, and don't cache user-specific data under a shared key.

2.16 How do you troubleshoot a slow API?

Work top-down and measure before changing anything:

	1.	Reproduce and scope: which endpoint, which percentile (p95/p99), since when, which users/data? Check recent deployments.
	2.	Observe: distributed tracing / APM (Application Insights, OpenTelemetry, Datadog), logs with correlation IDs, metrics (RPS, latency, error rate, CPU, memory, GC, thread-pool queue length).
	3.	Find the layer: network/gateway → app → downstream calls → database.
	4.	Database (most common culprit): slow query log, execution plan, missing/unused index, N+1, SELECT *, parameter sniffing, locking/blocking, large result sets, missing pagination.
	5.	Application: sync-over-async/thread-pool starvation, excessive allocations/GC, serialisation of large payloads, chatty downstream HTTP calls (parallelise, cache, batch), no HttpClientFactory, missing compression.
	6.	Infra: under-provisioned resources, cold starts, noisy neighbours, connection pool exhaustion.
	7.	Fix and verify: add index/rewrite query, cache, async/parallel, pagination, projection (Select DTOs, AsNoTracking), then load test (k6/JMeter/NBomber) to confirm the improvement, and add an alert so regressions are caught.

3. EF Core & Architecture

3.17 EF Core vs Dapper

	EF Core	Dapper
Type	Full ORM	Micro-ORM (maps SQL results to objects)
Productivity	High: LINQ, change tracking, migrations, relationships	You write SQL and map manually
Performance	Good; tracking overhead (mitigate with AsNoTracking, compiled queries)	Near raw ADO.NET
Control	Less control over generated SQL	Full control
Best for	CRUD, domain models, rapid development	Hot read paths, complex reports, bulk, stored procs

Senior answer: use both (CQRS-style): EF Core for writes and domain-driven transactional work; Dapper (or raw SQL/views) for performance-critical reads and reporting. They can share the same connection/transaction (context.Database.GetDbConnection()).

3.18 Eager vs lazy vs explicit loading

	•	Eager: load related data up front with Include/ThenInclude (single query or split with AsSplitQuery).
	•	Lazy: related data loads automatically on first navigation access (needs proxies + virtual navigation properties, or ILazyLoader). Convenient but a main source of N+1. Usually avoided in APIs.
	•	Explicit: you load on demand: context.Entry(order).Collection(o => o.Items).LoadAsync().

Depth: Prefer projections (Select into DTOs) which fetch only needed columns and avoid tracking altogether. Use AsSplitQuery to avoid cartesian explosion with multiple collection includes. AsNoTracking for read-only queries.

3.19 What is the N+1 query problem?

One query loads N parent rows, then N more queries run (one per parent) to fetch each child: 1 + N round trips. Typical cause: lazy loading or lazily accessing navigation properties inside a loop.

// N+1
var orders = await db.Orders.ToListAsync();
foreach (var o in orders) total += o.Items.Count;   // query per order with lazy loading

// Fix 1: eager load        db.Orders.Include(o => o.Items)
// Fix 2: project           db.Orders.Select(o => new { o.Id, ItemCount = o.Items.Count })
// Fix 3: batch             one query for children where OrderId IN (...), group in memory

Detect via SQL logging (LogTo), MiniProfiler, Application Insights, or an EF interceptor with a query-count threshold in tests.

3.20 Repository Pattern

An abstraction between the domain/business layer and data access that exposes collection-like operations (Add, GetById, Find) so business code doesn't depend on the persistence technology, and makes unit testing easier.

Senior nuance (be honest in interviews): DbContext already is a Unit of Work and DbSet<T> already behaves like a repository. A generic repository on top of EF is often an unnecessary leaky abstraction (it hides Include, projections, and so on). It still has value when you need: a domain-driven aggregate-root repository with intention-revealing methods (GetOpenInvoicesForCustomer), the ability to swap persistence, or strict layering. Alternatives: the Specification pattern, or injecting the DbContext directly into query handlers (CQRS). Pair repositories with a Unit of Work (SaveChangesAsync) for transactional consistency.

3.21 What is CQRS?

Command Query Responsibility Segregation: separate the model for writes (commands) that change state from the model for reads (queries) that return data.

	•	Commands: validated, run domain logic, transactional, return little/nothing.
	•	Queries: no side effects, optimised for reads (projections, Dapper, read replicas, denormalised views/search index).

Why: independent scaling and optimisation of reads vs writes, simpler handlers, clear intent. Implementation in .NET: MediatR (or hand-rolled handlers) with IRequest<T> and pipeline behaviours (validation, logging, transaction). Levels: same DB with separate models, then separate read store with sync via events (eventual consistency), and optionally Event Sourcing. Trade-off: more code and complexity; don't use it for simple CRUD. (Note: MediatR became commercial for new major versions, so mention alternatives like Wolverine/Mediator source generators or plain handlers.)

3.22 Clean Architecture

Concentric layers with the dependency rule: dependencies point inward only.

	1.	Domain (centre): entities, value objects, domain events, business rules; no dependencies.
	2.	Application: use cases (commands/queries/handlers), interfaces (IOrderRepository, IEmailSender), DTOs, validation.
	3.	Infrastructure: EF Core, repositories, external APIs, email, file storage; implements Application interfaces.
	4.	Presentation / API: controllers, minimal APIs, Angular client; composition root (DI wiring).

Benefits: testable business logic, framework/DB independence, maintainability. Costs: more projects, mapping and boilerplate. Practical advice: adopt it for long-lived, complex domains; for small CRUD apps use a simpler vertical-slice architecture. Combine it with DDD (aggregates), CQRS, and architecture tests (NetArchTest) to enforce layer rules.

4. Angular & TypeScript (Accenture list)

4.23 Explain OnPush change detection

See also §5 Q2–Q3 for the full comparison. In short:

	•	ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default: the component is checked on every change-detection cycle triggered by Zone.js (any event, timer, XHR).
	•	OnPush: the component (and its subtree) is checked only when: (1) an @Input reference changes, (2) an event originates in the component or its template, (3) an async pipe emits / a signal read in the template changes, or (4) markForCheck() / detectChanges() is called.
	•	Needs immutable data updates. Result: far fewer checks, big performance win in large trees. It is the recommended default for all components.

4.24 Promises vs Observables

See §5 Q5 for the full table. Summary: Promises are eager, single-value, non-cancellable. Observables are lazy, can emit multiple values over time, cancellable (unsubscribe), and come with a rich operator library (map, switchMap, debounceTime, retry...).

4.25 switchMap vs mergeMap vs concatMap vs exhaustMap

All map each source value to an inner observable and flatten the result. They differ in how they handle overlapping inner observables:

Operator	Behaviour	Order	Use case
switchMap	Cancels the previous inner when a new value arrives	latest wins	Typeahead search, route-param driven loads, "get latest"
mergeMap	Runs all inners concurrently	not guaranteed	Independent parallel calls, fire-and-forget logging, file uploads
concatMap	Queues; runs one at a time in order	preserved	Sequential saves where order matters (e.g. updating the same record)
exhaustMap	Ignores new values while an inner is active	first wins	Login/submit button (prevent double-submit), refresh

search$.pipe(switchMap(q => this.http.get(`/api/s?q=${q}`)));      // cancel stale
clicks$.pipe(exhaustMap(() => this.http.post('/api/pay', body)));  // ignore double click

Gotchas: switchMap on writes (POST/PUT) can cancel a request that already reached the server, so use concatMap/exhaustMap for mutations. mergeMap accepts a concurrent limit parameter.

4.26 Interceptors and route guards

HTTP interceptors sit between the app and the network: they can clone/modify every request and response. Uses: attach the JWT, global error handling (401 → refresh token/redirect, 5xx → toast), loading spinner, logging, caching, retry, correlation IDs. Modern Angular uses functional interceptors:

export const authInterceptor: HttpInterceptorFn = (req, next) => {
  const token = inject(AuthService).token();
  return next(token ? req.clone({ setHeaders: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } }) : req);
};
provideHttpClient(withInterceptors([authInterceptor]));

Careful with the refresh-token flow: queue concurrent 401s so only one refresh happens (exhaustMap/shared observable).

Route guards decide whether navigation is allowed: canActivate, canActivateChild, canDeactivate (unsaved changes), canMatch (controls lazy route matching/loading), plus resolve for pre-fetching data. Functional guards:

export const authGuard: CanActivateFn = (route, state) =>
  inject(AuthService).isLoggedIn() || inject(Router).createUrlTree(['/login'], { queryParams: { returnUrl: state.url } });

Remember: client-side guards are UX only, so real security is enforced on the API.

4.27 How do you optimise Angular performance?

	•	Change detection: OnPush everywhere, signals, avoid function calls in templates (use pure pipes/computed), consider zoneless.
	•	Rendering: @for with track (or trackBy), virtual scrolling (CDK), pagination, @defer blocks for lazy template sections.
	•	Loading: lazy-load routes/components, preloading strategies, NgOptimizedImage, SSR/hydration for first paint, code splitting.
	•	Bundle: production builds with AOT + tree-shaking, analyse with source-map-explorer/webpack-bundle-analyzer, import only what you use (e.g. lodash-es), budgets in angular.json, remove unused deps.
	•	RxJS: unsubscribe (takeUntilDestroyed, async pipe), shareReplay to avoid duplicate HTTP calls, debounce inputs.
	•	Network: HTTP caching headers, compression (brotli), pagination/filters on the server, CDN.
	•	Diagnose first: Angular DevTools profiler, Chrome Performance tab, Lighthouse, Core Web Vitals.
	•	Run heavy computation in Web Workers; run non-UI event listeners outside the Angular zone (runOutsideAngular).

4.28 Reactive forms vs template-driven forms

See §5 Q6 for the full table. Summary: reactive forms define the model in TypeScript (FormGroup/FormControl, typed forms), are explicit, scalable, testable, and ideal for complex/dynamic forms. Template-driven forms rely on ngModel in the template; fine for tiny, simple forms.

4.29 How do you manage shared state?

Choose the lightest tool that works:

	1.	@Input/@Output for parent–child only.
	2.	Service with signals (or BehaviorSubject) scoped to a feature or root: the default for most apps.
	3.	NgRx (Store, Effects, Selectors, Entity) or NgRx SignalStore for large apps with complex cross-feature state, time-travel debugging, and strict unidirectional flow. Alternatives: NGXS, Akita, TanStack Query for server state.
	4.	Router state/query params for shareable state (filters, pagination); URL as the source of truth.
	5.	Browser storage for persistence (with care).

Principles: single source of truth, immutable updates, derived state via computed/selectors, keep server state (cache, refetch, invalidation) separate from UI state. Don't introduce NgRx before you feel the pain.

4.30 How do you prevent memory leaks in Angular?

Causes: un-unsubscribed Observables (interval, fromEvent, WebSocket, store selects, valueChanges), manual event listeners/setInterval, retained references in singleton services, detached DOM, un-destroyed dynamic components.

Prevention:

	•	Prefer the async pipe (auto-unsubscribes) or toSignal.
	•	takeUntilDestroyed() (Angular 16+; pass DestroyRef outside injection context):

constructor() { this.data$.pipe(takeUntilDestroyed()).subscribe(...); }

	•	Older pattern: private destroy$ = new Subject<void>(), takeUntil(this.destroy$), complete in ngOnDestroy.
	•	Finite streams (HTTP) complete on their own; infinite ones don't.
	•	Clear timers; remove listeners (Renderer2.listen return fn); destroy dynamic ComponentRefs.
	•	Detect with Chrome DevTools heap snapshots (look for detached DOM nodes and growing listener counts) and Angular DevTools.

5. Angular Interview Round (Q1–Q17)

Q1. Introduce yourself

Template (≈60–90 sec). Adapt the bracketed parts:

"I'm [Name], a full-stack engineer with about 10 years of experience, primarily on the .NET and Angular stack. I currently work at [Company] as [Senior/Lead Engineer] where I [own/lead] [domain, e.g. logistics/finance platform]. On the backend I build ASP.NET Core Web APIs with EF Core/Dapper and SQL Server, using Clean Architecture, CQRS and async messaging [Azure Service Bus/RabbitMQ]. On the frontend I build Angular apps (standalone components, RxJS, signals, NgRx) with a focus on performance and maintainability.
