Q2. Default vs OnPush change detection

Short: Angular must keep the view in sync with data. With Default, it re-checks the entire component tree after every async event (via Zone.js). With OnPush, a component is checked only when its inputs change by reference, an event fires inside it, an observable bound with async emits (or a signal it reads changes), or it is explicitly marked dirty.

	Default	OnPush
Checked	Every CD cycle	Only when "dirty"
Input change detection	Any mutation visible	Reference change only
Performance	Fine for small apps	Much better for large trees
Data style	Mutable OK	Immutable updates / signals / observables

@Component({ changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush, ... })

Depth: OnPush plus immutability plus async pipe/signals is the standard for scalable apps. The subtree below an OnPush component is skipped unless it is itself dirty; markForCheck() marks the component and its ancestors dirty.

Q3. Will modifying a property inside an existing object trigger OnPush?

No. If a parent passes an object to an OnPush child and then mutates a property (this.user.name = 'X'), the reference is unchanged, so Angular doesn't see an input change and the child view won't update (unless another trigger occurs, e.g. an event inside the child).

Fixes:

	•	Replace the reference (immutability): this.user = { ...this.user, name: 'X' }; (arrays: spread/map/filter, not push).
	•	Use signals (user.update(u => ({ ...u, name: 'X' }))) or async pipe with observables.
	•	Last resort: cdr.markForCheck() (or detectChanges()).

Nuance: if the mutation happens in an event handler inside the OnPush component itself, the view is refreshed because the event marks it dirty.

Q4. Different ways of sharing data between two components

Parent ↔ child

	•	@Input() (parent → child), @Output() + EventEmitter (child → parent); the modern input(), output(), model() (two-way) signal APIs.
	•	@ViewChild/@ViewChildren/viewChild() (parent reads the child instance), template reference variables.
	•	Content projection (<ng-content>).

Unrelated / siblings / across the app

	•	Shared service holding state: BehaviorSubject/Subject (RxJS) or signals.
	•	State management libs: NgRx / NgRx SignalStore / NGXS.
	•	Router: route params, query params, route data, state in navigate.
	•	Browser storage: localStorage/sessionStorage/cookies (persistence, with care).
	•	Dependency injection with scoped providers (component-level providers), InjectionTokens.
	•	BroadcastChannel/postMessage across tabs/iframes (special cases).

Rule of thumb: inputs/outputs for direct relations, a service (signals) for siblings/feature-wide, NgRx for complex app-wide state.

Q5. Observable vs Promise

	Promise	Observable (RxJS)
Values	One value (or error)	0..n values over time
Execution	Eager: runs immediately	Lazy: runs on subscribe
Cancellation	No (native)	Yes (unsubscribe, takeUntil, switchMap)
Operators	then/catch/finally	100+ (map, filter, debounceTime, retry, combineLatest...)
Multicasting	Always shared	Cold by default; share/shareReplay/Subjects to multicast
Sync/async	Always async (microtask)	Can be sync or async
Use	One-shot async ops	Streams: events, WebSockets, form value changes, polling, HTTP in Angular

Interop: firstValueFrom(obs$) / lastValueFrom, from(promise). Angular's HttpClient returns cold observables, so nothing happens until you subscribe and subscribing twice sends two requests.

