Senior notes: valueChanges + debounceTime for live validation, updateOn: 'blur', custom/async validators, ControlValueAccessor for custom controls, getRawValue() vs value.

Q7. Advantage of lazy loading + syntax

Advantage: the initial bundle contains only what the first route needs; feature code downloads on demand. Result: faster initial load/TTI, smaller main bundle, better caching, and users who never visit a section don't pay for it.

Standalone component (modern, Angular 14+):

// app.routes.ts
export const routes: Routes = [
  { path: 'dashboard',
    loadComponent: () => import('./dashboard/dashboard.component').then(m => m.DashboardComponent) },
  { path: 'admin',
    canMatch: [adminGuard],
    loadChildren: () => import('./admin/admin.routes').then(m => m.ADMIN_ROUTES) }
];

NgModule-based (legacy):

{ path: 'orders', loadChildren: () => import('./orders/orders.module').then(m => m.OrdersModule) }

Extras: preloading (withPreloading(PreloadAllModules) or a custom strategy), @defer (on viewport) for lazy-loading parts of a template, and canMatch to avoid even downloading a route chunk for unauthorised users.

Q8. Which is the default component in Angular?

AppComponent: the root component generated by the CLI (selector: 'app-root'). It is bootstrapped in main.ts (bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, appConfig), or AppModule's bootstrap: [AppComponent] in older projects), and index.html contains <app-root></app-root>. In newer CLI versions the class may be named App in app.ts, but the role is the same.

Q9. Can I bootstrap a custom dashboard component instead of AppComponent?

Yes, it works. Any component can be the root component as long as it's standalone (or declared in the module), and index.html contains its selector:

bootstrapApplication(DashboardComponent, appConfig);   // main.ts

<app-dashboard></app-dashboard>   <!-- index.html instead of <app-root> -->

You can even bootstrap multiple root components in an NgModule's bootstrap: [A, B], or create components dynamically with createComponent (e.g. Angular Elements/micro-frontends).

Q10. Is it good practice or should we avoid it?

Generally avoid it for normal SPAs.

	•	Convention: AppComponent is a thin shell (layout, header, <router-outlet>); features such as the dashboard are routes (/dashboard) that can be lazy-loaded, guarded, deep-linked, and have resolvers. Bootstrapping the dashboard directly bypasses that structure, makes routing/layout awkward, and surprises teammates and tooling (CLI, tests, docs).
	•	Hurts: guards and global layout (nav, error handling) have no natural home; harder to add a second page later.

Legitimate exceptions: a single-purpose embeddable widget, micro-frontend remotes, Angular Elements (custom elements) on non-Angular pages, multiple independent Angular apps on one page.

Answer: "It works technically, but I'd keep AppComponent as the shell and route to the dashboard, unless it's a deliberate widget/micro-frontend scenario."

Q11. Search box calls API on every keystroke: prevent it, with code

Use debounceTime + distinctUntilChanged + switchMap:

searchCtrl = new FormControl('', { nonNullable: true });

results$ = this.searchCtrl.valueChanges.pipe(
  map(v => v.trim()),
  debounceTime(300),                 // wait for the user to pause typing
  distinctUntilChanged(),            // skip identical consecutive values
  filter(v => v.length >= 2),        // optional min length
  switchMap(q =>
    this.http.get<Item[]>('/api/items', { params: { q } }).pipe(
      catchError(() => of([]))       // keep the stream alive on error
    )),
);

<input [formControl]="searchCtrl" />
@for (r of results$ | async; track r.id) { <li>{{ r.name }}</li> }

Why each operator: debounceTime cuts request volume, distinctUntilChanged avoids duplicates, switchMap cancels the in-flight request so stale responses never overwrite newer ones. Mention server-side rate limiting and caching as complements.

Signals version (Angular 17+/RxJS interop):

query = signal('');
results = toSignal(
  toObservable(this.query).pipe(debounceTime(300), distinctUntilChanged(),
    switchMap(q => this.api.search(q))), { initialValue: [] });

(Angular 19+ also offers rxResource/resource for async signal-based loading.)

Q12. What does switchMap do? What is its use?

switchMap maps each emission of the source to a new inner observable and subscribes to the latest one, unsubscribing from the previous inner observable if it hasn't completed. Only results from the most recent inner observable are emitted.

Uses: typeahead/search, loading data when a route param changes (route.paramMap.pipe(switchMap(p => api.get(p.get('id'))))), dependent dropdowns, auto-refresh/polling (restart on trigger). It prevents race conditions and wasted work because the HTTP request is cancelled (browser aborts XHR).
Don't use for non-idempotent writes where cancelling mid-flight is dangerous; use concatMap/exhaustMap (see §4.25).

