Q14. What are signals?

A signal is a reactive wrapper around a value that notifies consumers when it changes. Angular tracks which signals a template/computation reads, so it can update only the affected parts (fine-grained reactivity) and enables zoneless change detection.

count = signal(0);                       // writable
double = computed(() => this.count() * 2); // derived, lazy, memoised, read-only
constructor() { effect(() => console.log(this.count())); } // side effects

this.count.set(5);
this.count.update(c => c + 1);

	•	Read by calling it: count().
	•	computed: automatic dependency tracking, cached until dependencies change.
	•	effect: for side effects (logging, syncing to storage), not for deriving state (use computed); avoid writing signals inside effects.
	•	Signal-based APIs: input(), output(), model(), viewChild(), toSignal(), toObservable(), linkedSignal(), resource().
	•	Always synchronous and always have a value (unlike observables). Equality check by reference (Object.is) → use immutable updates.

Q15. Can we use signals for sharing data between multiple components?

Yes. Put the signal in an injectable service (providedIn: 'root' for app-wide, or component-level providers for scoped state). Every component that injects the service reads the same signal and updates automatically.

@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class CartStore {
  private _items = signal<Item[]>([]);
  readonly items = this._items.asReadonly();         // expose read-only
  readonly total = computed(() => this._items().reduce((s, i) => s + i.price, 0));
  add(i: Item) { this._items.update(list => [...list, i]); }
}

Components: cart = inject(CartStore) then {{ cart.total() }} in the template. Keep writes behind service methods (encapsulation). For larger needs, use NgRx SignalStore.

Q16. A value (a signal) in the employee component changes every 10–20 min. Will you prefer signals for sharing that data?

Yes, but how often it changes isn't the deciding factor; where the data comes from is.

	•	10–20 minutes is a very low update frequency, so signals are perfectly fine. Even high-frequency updates are OK because signals notify only dependents.
	•	If the value is produced in the component/is synchronous UI state: keep it as a signal and share it through a service (don't make other components reach into the employee component).
	•	If it comes from asynchronous sources (polling, WebSocket/SignalR push, HTTP refresh), keep the stream in RxJS (timer/interval + switchMap, or a SignalR subscription), then convert to a signal for consumption: toSignal(stream$). RxJS is better for time-based and cancellation logic; signals are better for state consumed by the template.
	•	Place the state in a shared service/store, not in the employee component, so consumers don't depend on a component's lifecycle.
	•	With OnPush/zoneless apps, signals read in templates refresh the view automatically with no manual markForCheck.

Q17. Difference between let and const in JavaScript

See §6.7. Short version: both are block-scoped and hoisted into the TDZ. let can be reassigned; const cannot be re-assigned and must be initialised at declaration, but a const object/array is still mutable internally (use Object.freeze or immutable patterns for true immutability).

6. JavaScript

6.1 Spread vs Rest operators

Both use ...; the position determines the meaning.

	•	Spread expands an iterable/object into individual elements (call sites, array/object literals).
	•	Rest collects remaining elements into an array/object (function parameters, destructuring).

// Spread
const a = [1, 2], b = [...a, 3];              // [1,2,3]
const o2 = { ...{ x: 1, y: 2 }, y: 9 };       // { x:1, y:9 }  (shallow copy + override)
Math.max(...a);

// Rest
function sum(first, ...rest) { return rest.reduce((s, n) => s + n, first); }
const [head, ...tail] = [1, 2, 3];            // head=1, tail=[2,3]
const { id, ...others } = { id: 1, a: 2, b: 3 };

Note: spread makes shallow copies only (nested objects keep shared references; use structuredClone for deep copies). The rest parameter must be last.

6.2 Closures: disadvantages and memory leaks

A closure is a function bundled with references to its lexical scope, so it can access outer variables even after the outer function has returned.

function counter() { let c = 0; return () => ++c; }
const next = counter(); next(); // 1

Uses: data privacy/encapsulation, factories, memoisation, currying, callbacks/event handlers, module pattern.

Disadvantages:

	•	Captured variables stay in memory as long as the closure is reachable. A big captured object/DOM node is retained even if only a small part is used.
	•	Memory leaks examples: event listeners never removed (the closure keeps the DOM node and its data alive), setInterval/setTimeout callbacks never cleared, closures stored in global caches/maps, detached DOM nodes referenced by closures, RxJS subscriptions holding component references.
	•	The classic loop bug with var: all callbacks share one variable (let creates a new binding per iteration).
	•	Slightly harder to debug.

Mitigation: remove listeners (removeEventListener, AbortController), clearInterval, null out references, capture only what's needed, use WeakMap/WeakRef for caches, and profile with the Chrome heap snapshot.

6.3 What is an IIFE? Why use it?

Immediately Invoked Function Expression: a function defined and executed at once.

(function () { const secret = 1; /* ... */ })();
(() => { /* ... */ })();
(async () => { await main(); })();      // async IIFE (top-level await alternative)

Why: create a private scope (avoid polluting globals / name collisions), the module pattern with private state (pre-ES modules), run initialisation code once, and capture loop variables (old var fix). Today: ES modules and block scope (let/const) have mostly replaced the need, but async IIFEs are still handy.

6.4 Debouncing vs Throttling

	Debounce	Throttle
Behaviour	Run after events stop for N ms (the timer resets on each event)	Run at most once every N ms while events keep coming
Think	"Wait until the user finishes"	"Limit the rate"
Use cases	Search-as-you-type, auto-save, window-resize end, form validation	Scroll/mousemove handlers, infinite scroll, drag, button spam protection, analytics pings

const debounce = (fn, ms) => { let t; return (...a) => { clearTimeout(t); t = setTimeout(() => fn(...a), ms); }; };
const throttle = (fn, ms) => { let last = 0; return (...a) => { const now = Date.now(); if (now - last >= ms) { last = now; fn(...a); } }; };

RxJS equivalents: debounceTime, throttleTime, auditTime, sampleTime. Variants: leading/trailing edge execution.

6.5 Hoisting with var, let, const; the Temporal Dead Zone

Hoisting: declarations are processed before code runs (during the creation phase).

	•	var: hoisted and initialised to undefined, function-scoped. Accessing it before the assignment gives undefined.
	•	let / const: hoisted but not initialised; they sit in the Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ) from the start of the block until the declaration line executes. Accessing them in the TDZ throws ReferenceError.
	•	Function declarations are hoisted fully (callable before definition); function expressions/arrow functions follow their variable's rule. class declarations are also in the TDZ.

console.log(a); var a = 1;      // undefined
console.log(b); let b = 2;      // ReferenceError (TDZ)
typeof c; const c = 3;          // ReferenceError: typeof isn't safe in the TDZ
foo(); function foo() {}        // works
bar(); var bar = () => {};      // TypeError: bar is not a function

The TDZ catches use-before-define bugs.

6.6 Output-based questions: execution order

Key model: call stack → microtask queue (Promise .then/.catch/.finally, await continuations, queueMicrotask, MutationObserver) → macrotask queue (setTimeout, setInterval, I/O, UI events). After the synchronous code finishes, the event loop drains all microtasks before taking the next macrotask. (process.nextTick in Node runs even before promise microtasks.)

Q A

console.log('1');
setTimeout(() => console.log('2'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('3'));
console.log('4');

Output: 1, 4, 3, 2.

Q B (async/await)

async function f() { console.log('a'); await null; console.log('b'); }
console.log('c'); f(); console.log('d');

Output: c, a, d, b (code after await is a microtask).

Q C (mix)

setTimeout(() => console.log('timeout'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('p1')).then(() => console.log('p2'));
queueMicrotask(() => console.log('micro'));
console.log('sync');

Output: sync, p1, micro, p2, timeout. (p1 and micro are queued first; p2 is queued when p1 resolves, after micro.)

Q D (var in loops: the classic)

for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);   // 3 3 3
for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) setTimeout(() => console.log(j), 0);   // 0 1 2

Q E (this/hoisting)

var x = 1; function t() { console.log(x); var x = 2; } t();        // undefined (local x hoisted)

Approach in the interview: write the three queues on paper, run sync code first, then drain microtasks, then one macrotask, repeat.

6.7 let vs const (also var)

	var	let	const
Scope	Function	Block	Block
Hoisting	Yes, initialised to undefined	Yes, TDZ	Yes, TDZ
Re-declare in same scope	Allowed	No	No
Re-assign	Yes	Yes	No
Must initialise	No	No	Yes
Global object property	Yes (global scope)	No	No

const prevents re-binding, not mutation: const o = {a:1}; o.a = 2; is fine; o = {} throws. Use Object.freeze() (shallow) for immutability. Best practice: default to const, use let when reassigning, avoid var.

7. Angular Extras

7.1 What is Zone.js and how does Angular use it for change detection?

Zone.js monkey-patches async browser APIs (setTimeout, Promises, DOM events, XHR/fetch) to create an execution context ("zone") that can intercept when async work starts and finishes. Angular runs your code inside NgZone; when the zone becomes stable (the task queue is empty after an async task), NgZone.onMicrotaskEmpty fires and Angular runs change detection from the root (ApplicationRef.tick()), checking bindings top-down. That's why the UI updates "magically" after an HTTP response or click.

Implications:

	•	Every async event triggers a CD pass, even if nothing changed, so there's a cost; mitigate with OnPush.
	•	Frequent events that don't affect UI (scroll, mousemove, setInterval for charts, third-party libs) → run outside the zone: ngZone.runOutsideAngular(() => ...), then re-enter with ngZone.run.
	•	Zoneless (provideZonelessChangeDetection()): Angular uses signals/markForCheck/events as the notification mechanism instead of Zone.js. Benefits: smaller bundles, better performance and debugging, and compatibility with native async/await. Needs signal/OnPush-friendly code.

7.2 Angular Signals vs RxJS Observables

	Signals	Observables
Model	State/value (always has a current value)	Stream of events over time
Reading	Synchronous sig()	subscribe / async pipe
Initial value	Required	Optional (BehaviorSubject has one)
Async/time-based ops	Not built in	Excellent (debounceTime, retry, switchMap, combineLatest, ...)
Cleanup	Automatic (framework-managed graph)	Manual unsubscribe (or async/takeUntilDestroyed)
Change detection	Fine-grained, glitch-free, enables zoneless	Needs async pipe/markForCheck
Best for	UI/synchronous state, derived values	Events, HTTP, WebSockets, complex async flows

They complement each other. Observables for async pipelines → convert with toSignal() for templates; toObservable() to feed a signal into an RxJS pipeline. Rule: RxJS for events and async, signals for state.

7.3 Why create a custom Pipe? Pure vs impure

Why: reusable, declarative template transformations (formatting dates/currency/phone numbers, truncating, filtering/sorting display lists, highlighting search text, mapping enum → label) without cluttering components or calling methods in templates (methods run on every CD cycle).

	•	Pure pipe (default, pure: true): re-runs only when its input reference changes (or a primitive value changes). Fast, cached, ideal when it's a deterministic function of its inputs. Use for formatting and transformation; combine with immutable data.
	•	Impure pipe (pure: false): re-runs on every change-detection cycle. Use only when the result depends on something other than inputs (internal state, mutating arrays, time, async data like AsyncPipe). Performance risk, so keep it light or avoid it.

@Pipe({ name: 'truncate', standalone: true })      // pure by default
export class TruncatePipe implements PipeTransform {
  transform(v: string, max = 50) { return v.length > max ? v.slice(0, max) + '…' : v; }
}

Better than an impure filter pipe: filter in the component with computed()/RxJS and bind the result.

7.4 Scenario: API called repeatedly while the user types in a search box

Same as §5 Q11: debounceTime(300) + distinctUntilChanged() + switchMap (cancels stale requests), plus a minimum length, error handling inside the inner observable, optional client caching (shareReplay / a Map cache), and server-side rate limiting/response caching.

7.5 Scenario: component is slow when rendering thousands of records

Identify:

	•	Angular DevTools profiler (CD cycles per component), Chrome Performance panel (long tasks/scripting vs rendering), check the DOM node count and network payload size.
	•	Look for method calls in templates, missing trackBy, Default change detection, and heavy pipes.

Improve (in order of impact):

	1.	Don't render thousands: server-side pagination/filtering/sorting; infinite scroll in chunks.
	2.	Virtual scrolling (cdk-virtual-scroll-viewport) so only visible rows exist in the DOM.
	3.	OnPush + immutable data/signals.
	4.	@for (...; track item.id) (stable identity so rows are reused, not re-created).
	5.	Replace function calls in templates with computed/pure pipes; precompute derived fields.
	6.	Debounce filtering; run heavy transforms in a Web Worker.
	7.	Lighter DOM/CSS per row, @defer for expensive child widgets, NgOptimizedImage.
	8.	Reduce payload: send only needed fields, compress.
Verify with before/after profiler numbers.

7.6 Scenario: two unrelated components need to share data. Which approach and why?

A shared, injectable service holding the state, using signals (or a BehaviorSubject).

	•	No parent–child relationship, so @Input/@Output don't apply, and bubbling through ancestors is brittle.
	•	A root (or feature-scoped) service gives one source of truth, decoupling, testability, and it works naturally with OnPush/signals.
	•	For complex, app-wide state with many actions/side effects → NgRx / SignalStore.
	•	For shareable/bookmarkable state (filters, selected id) → URL query params; for persistence across reloads → storage.
Call out trade-offs: keep the service API small, expose read-only signals, avoid a "god service".

8. SQL Server

8.31 Clustered vs non-clustered indexes

	Clustered	Non-clustered
What	Defines the physical order of the table's data; the leaf level is the data pages	Separate structure with key columns + a row locator (clustering key, or RID for a heap) pointing to the data
Count per table	One	Many (up to 999)
Created by default by	PRIMARY KEY	UNIQUE constraint, or explicit CREATE INDEX
Best for	Range scans, ordered retrieval, narrow/unique/ever-increasing keys (e.g. IDENTITY int/bigint)	Selective lookups, covering queries, filtering/joining on non-key columns
Lookup cost	Direct	Index seek then key lookup (unless covering)

Depth: Choose a narrow, static, unique, ever-increasing clustered key (random GUIDs cause page splits and fragmentation; use NEWSEQUENTIALID() or UUIDv7-style). Every non-clustered index carries the clustering key, so a wide clustered key bloats all indexes. Use INCLUDE columns to make covering indexes and avoid key lookups, and filtered indexes for subsets. Over-indexing slows writes (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE maintain every index) and costs storage. Monitor with sys.dm_db_index_usage_stats, sys.dm_db_missing_index_details, and fragmentation (sys.dm_db_index_physical_stats: reorganise 5–30%, rebuild >30%). Columnstore for analytics.

8.1 Primary key vs unique key (from your first screenshot)

	•	Primary key: uniquely identifies each row; no NULLs; one per table; creates a clustered index by default.
	•	Unique key/constraint: enforces uniqueness on other columns; allows one NULL (SQL Server treats NULLs as equal for uniqueness); many per table; creates a non-clustered index by default. (To allow multiple NULLs, use a filtered unique index WHERE col IS NOT NULL.)

8.2 What is a composite key and why do we use it?

A composite key is a key made of two or more columns that together uniquely identify a row.

Why: when no single column is unique (e.g. a many-to-many junction table), to guarantee the combination is unique and prevent duplicate pairs.

CREATE TABLE StudentCourses (
    StudentId INT NOT NULL,
    CourseId  INT NOT NULL,
    EnrolledOn DATE NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSDATETIME(),
    CONSTRAINT PK_StudentCourses PRIMARY KEY (StudentId, CourseId),
    CONSTRAINT FK_SC_Student FOREIGN KEY (StudentId) REFERENCES Students(Id),
    CONSTRAINT FK_SC_Course  FOREIGN KEY (CourseId)  REFERENCES Courses(Id)
);

Note: column order matters for index usage (queries filtering on StudentId use the index; filtering only on CourseId won't seek efficiently, so add a separate index if needed). A surrogate single-column key is often simpler when other tables need to reference the row. EF Core: HasKey(x => new { x.StudentId, x.CourseId }).

8.3 EXISTS vs IN

	EXISTS	IN
Checks	Whether the subquery returns any row (TRUE/FALSE)	Whether a value matches any value in a list/subquery result
Subquery	Usually correlated; SELECT 1 is conventional (the select list is ignored)	Non-correlated, returns a single column
Short-circuit	Stops at the first match	Compares against the list (the optimizer may transform it)
NULL handling	Unaffected by NULLs in the subquery	NOT IN returns no rows if the subquery contains a NULL (three-valued logic trap)
Use	Existence checks, large subquery results	Small static lists, simple membership

SELECT c.* FROM Customers c
WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM Orders o WHERE o.CustomerId = c.Id);

SELECT * FROM Customers WHERE Id IN (SELECT CustomerId FROM Orders);
SELECT * FROM Customers WHERE Id IN (1, 2, 3);

Depth: In modern SQL Server the optimizer often produces the same plan (semi-join) for both. Performance depends on indexes, statistics and data, so check the execution plan rather than assume. Prefer NOT EXISTS over NOT IN because of NULL safety.

8.32 Find the Nth-highest salary

-- 1) DENSE_RANK (handles ties; best general answer)
SELECT DISTINCT Salary FROM (
  SELECT Salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY Salary DESC) AS rnk FROM Employees
) t WHERE rnk = @N;

-- 2) OFFSET/FETCH (distinct salaries)
SELECT DISTINCT Salary FROM Employees
ORDER BY Salary DESC OFFSET @N - 1 ROWS FETCH NEXT 1 ROWS ONLY;
-- note: DISTINCT with ORDER BY needs the column in select; wrap as
-- SELECT Salary FROM (SELECT DISTINCT Salary FROM Employees) d ORDER BY Salary DESC OFFSET @N-1 ROWS FETCH NEXT 1 ROW ONLY;

-- 3) Correlated subquery (classic, slower)
SELECT MAX(Salary) FROM Employees e1
WHERE @N - 1 = (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT Salary) FROM Employees e2 WHERE e2.Salary > e1.Salary);

-- 4) Per-department Nth
SELECT * FROM (
  SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY DeptId ORDER BY Salary DESC) rnk FROM Employees
) x WHERE rnk = @N;

Explain ROW_NUMBER vs RANK vs DENSE_RANK (ties/gaps), and that an index on Salary helps. Return NULL if there are fewer than N distinct salaries (wrap in a scalar subquery).

8.33 How do you analyse execution plans?

	1.	Capture the Actual Execution Plan (SSMS Ctrl+M; or SET STATISTICS IO, TIME ON; Query Store for historical plans; sys.dm_exec_query_stats).
	2.	Read right-to-left, top-to-bottom; look at operator cost % and the thick arrows (row counts).
	3.	Red flags:
	•	Table/Clustered Index Scan on a big table where a Seek is expected → missing/unusable index or non-SARGable predicate (functions on columns, leading-wildcard LIKE, implicit conversions).
	•	Key/RID Lookup repeated for many rows → make the index covering (INCLUDE).
	•	Estimated vs actual rows mismatch → stale statistics, parameter sniffing, table variables, or poor cardinality estimation.
	•	Sort/Hash spills to tempdb, large memory grants, expensive Nested Loops with big outer input, or Hash Match where a Merge/Seek would do.
	•	Warnings: implicit conversion, missing index hint, no join predicate.
	4.	Fix: indexes, rewrite the query (SARGable, avoid SELECT *, replace scalar UDFs/cursors with set-based logic), update statistics, OPTION (RECOMPILE) or OPTIMIZE FOR for parameter sniffing, Query Store plan forcing, partitioning.
	5.	Re-measure (logical reads, CPU, duration) to confirm.

8.34 What causes deadlocks, and how do you resolve them?

Deadlock: two (or more) sessions each hold a lock the other needs, so neither can proceed. SQL Server's deadlock monitor picks a victim (error 1205) and rolls it back.

Common causes: transactions accessing resources in different orders, long-running transactions, missing indexes (scans lock more rows/pages), lock escalation, high isolation levels (SERIALIZABLE/REPEATABLE READ), read-then-update (shared→exclusive upgrade) patterns, and user interaction inside a transaction.

Diagnose: system_health extended events / deadlock graph XML, trace flag 1222, SQL Profiler, sp_whoisactive.

Resolve/prevent:

	•	Access objects in a consistent order; keep transactions short, no user input inside.
	•	Add proper indexes so queries touch fewer rows.
	•	Use READ_COMMITTED_SNAPSHOT/Snapshot isolation (row versioning) so readers don't block writers.
	•	Use UPDLOCK hints on read-then-update patterns; lower lock granularity or use ROWLOCK carefully.
	•	Retry logic in the app for error 1205 (with backoff) (EF Core EnableRetryOnFailure or Polly).
	•	Batch large updates; avoid cursors.

8.35 Transactions and isolation levels

Transaction: a unit of work that is ACID: Atomic (all or nothing), Consistent (valid state to valid state), Isolated (concurrent transactions don't interfere improperly), Durable (committed data survives crashes via the write-ahead log).

BEGIN TRY
  BEGIN TRAN; /* work */ COMMIT;
END TRY
BEGIN CATCH
  IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0 ROLLBACK; THROW;
END CATCH
-- SET XACT_ABORT ON recommended

Concurrency problems: dirty read (reading uncommitted data), non-repeatable read (same row read twice gives different values), phantom read (a re-run range query returns new rows), lost update.

Isolation level	Dirty read	Non-repeatable	Phantom	Notes
READ UNCOMMITTED (NOLOCK)	Yes	Yes	Yes	Fastest, unreliable data
READ COMMITTED (default)	No	Yes	Yes	Shared locks released after read; with RCSI uses row versions
REPEATABLE READ	No	No	Yes	Holds shared locks until commit
SERIALIZABLE	No	No	No	Range locks; highest blocking/deadlock risk
SNAPSHOT	No	No	No	Row versioning; optimistic; update-conflict errors possible

Senior points: prefer RCSI for read-heavy OLTP; NOLOCK is a smell, not a fix; keep transactions short; distributed transactions (MSDTC) are costly, so prefer Outbox/Saga in microservices. In EF Core: SaveChanges is transactional by default; use BeginTransactionAsync for multi-step work; optimistic concurrency with [Timestamp]/rowversion and DbUpdateConcurrencyException.

9. System Design & Real-World Scenarios

9.36 How would you design a scalable .NET + Angular application?

Approach in an interview: clarify requirements first (users, RPS, data volume, latency/availability targets, consistency needs, budget), then walk through layers.

Architecture (cloud, e.g. Azure):

Browser (Angular SPA, CDN / Static Web Apps)
   → WAF / API Gateway (YARP/APIM/Front Door): TLS, auth, rate limiting, routing
   → Load balancer → stateless ASP.NET Core API instances (containers on AKS/App Service, autoscaling)
        → Cache (Redis)
        → SQL Server / Azure SQL (primary + read replicas), optional NoSQL/Search (Cosmos, Elasticsearch)
        → Message broker (Service Bus/RabbitMQ/Kafka) → background workers (BackgroundService / Functions)
        → Blob storage (files)
   Cross-cutting: Identity provider (OIDC/JWT), Key Vault, OpenTelemetry + App Insights/Grafana, CI/CD

Key decisions:

	•	Stateless services (no in-memory sessions; JWT; Redis for shared state) → horizontal scaling and zero-downtime deploys.
	•	Modular monolith first, split into microservices only where scale/team boundaries demand it. Clean Architecture/DDD, CQRS where reads and writes differ.
	•	Database: indexing, partitioning, read replicas for reporting, connection pooling, avoid N+1, async everywhere; consider sharding only when necessary.
	•	Caching layers: CDN → HTTP/response cache → Redis → DB. Define invalidation.
	•	Async processing: queue long tasks (emails, reports, invoice generation), use the Outbox pattern for reliable events, idempotent consumers, dead-letter queues.
	•	Resilience: Polly (retry with jitter, circuit breaker, timeout, bulkhead), health checks, graceful degradation, rate limiting.
	•	Frontend: lazy-loaded routes, OnPush/signals, SSR where SEO matters, CDN, bundle budgets.
	•	Ops: Docker, Kubernetes/App Service autoscale, IaC (Bicep/Terraform), CI/CD with automated tests, blue-green/canary, structured logging + distributed tracing + SLO-based alerts.
	•	Security: OAuth2/OIDC, least privilege, secrets in Key Vault, OWASP hardening, encryption at rest and in transit.
	•	Trade-offs: CAP/consistency vs availability, cost vs performance, complexity vs team size.

9.37 Prevent duplicate invoice generation under concurrent requests

The goal is idempotency and mutual exclusion at the data layer, because app-level checks alone race (two requests both check "does it exist?" then both insert).

Layers of defence (use several):

	1.	Database unique constraint (the ultimate guard): unique index on the business key, e.g. (OrderId) or (CustomerId, BillingPeriod). The second insert fails with a unique violation (2627/2601) → catch and return the existing invoice.

CREATE UNIQUE INDEX UX_Invoice_Order ON Invoices(OrderId);

	2.	Idempotency key: client sends an Idempotency-Key header (GUID) per logical operation; the server stores (key → response/invoiceId) with a unique constraint, and replays the stored result for retries/double-clicks. Stripe-style.
	3.	Transaction + locking for check-then-act: SELECT ... WITH (UPDLOCK, HOLDLOCK) on the order row (or sp_getapplock), or an optimistic rowversion check on the Order (DbUpdateConcurrencyException), or a state transition guard: UPDATE Orders SET Status='Invoiced' WHERE Id=@id AND Status='Ready' and check @@ROWCOUNT = 1 before creating the invoice (atomic compare-and-set).
	4.	Distributed lock (Redis SET NX PX/RedLock, or a SQL app lock) when multiple app instances must serialise a section; always with a TTL.
	5.	Messaging: use a queue with a single consumer per order key (Service Bus sessions/partition key), idempotent handlers, and the inbox/outbox pattern to deduplicate.
	6.	Frontend: disable the button while pending (exhaustMap) as a UX nicety only, never as the protection.
	7.	Make invoice number generation atomic (SQL SEQUENCE), and add tests that fire parallel requests.

9.38 Optimise pagination for millions of records

Problem with OFFSET/FETCH (or Skip/Take): SQL Server must scan and discard all the skipped rows, so deep pages get slower (page 100,000 is very slow). COUNT(*) on every request is also expensive.

Optimisations:

	•	Keyset (seek) pagination: use the last row's key instead of an offset. Stable and O(log n):

SELECT TOP (@PageSize) Id, CreatedAt, ...
FROM Orders
WHERE (CreatedAt < @LastCreatedAt) OR (CreatedAt = @LastCreatedAt AND Id < @LastId)
ORDER BY CreatedAt DESC, Id DESC;

Supported by an index on (CreatedAt DESC, Id DESC). Return a cursor/continuation token to the client (infinite scroll/next-prev). Limitation: no random jump to page N.

	•	Index the sort/filter columns; make it covering (INCLUDE) so there are no key lookups; always include a unique tiebreaker in ORDER BY for deterministic ordering.
	•	Project only needed columns (Select DTO), AsNoTracking, no Include of big graphs.
	•	Avoid exact COUNT(*): use an approximate total (sys.dm_db_partition_stats), cache the count, show "10,000+", or compute it asynchronously.
	•	If page-number UI is mandatory: limit max pages, use the "deferred join" trick (page over ids from a narrow index then join for the full rows), or move search/listing to a search engine (Elasticsearch/Azure AI Search).
	•	Cap pageSize server-side; cache hot pages; use read replicas; partition huge tables by date.
	•	Angular: virtual scroll + server-driven cursor, debounced filters.

9.39 Describe a production issue you solved and how you found the root cause

Use STAR (Situation, Task, Action, Result) plus a blameless RCA mindset. Replace the example with your real story (it must be true and defensible).

Generic full-stack example (slow screen in production, .NET API + Angular 19):

	•	Situation: A week after a release, support reported that the Orders list screen was taking 10–15 seconds to load for some customers, and a few requests were timing out. Application Insights showed the p95 of GET /api/orders had jumped from ~500 ms to 12 s, and DB CPU was spiking.
	•	Task: I owned the module, so I was responsible for restoring performance and finding the root cause.
	•	Action:
	1.	Triage: checked the scope. Only large customers (50k+ orders) were affected, and it started with the last release. We added a temporary page-size cap via config to reduce impact while investigating.
	2.	Trace the layers: the Angular network tab and App Insights traces showed the API call itself was slow (not rendering). The same request was also being fired twice.
	3.	Database: SQL logging and Query Store showed one query doing a full scan plus 1 + N follow-up queries per order (EF Core lazy loading of order items). Execution plan: clustered index scan, key lookups, and a non-SARGable WHERE YEAR(CreatedAt) = @y.
	4.	Frontend: the component subscribed to the same HttpClient observable twice (once with async pipe, once manually in ngOnInit), and the search box had no debounceTime, so each keystroke caused a new request.
	•	Root cause: a combination of a non-SARGable filter, a missing composite index, N+1 from lazy loading, and duplicate/undebounced requests from the UI.
	•	Fix:
	•	API: rewrote the filter as a date range, added a covering index on (CustomerId, CreatedAt) INCLUDE (...), replaced lazy loading with a Select projection to a DTO plus AsNoTracking, and added keyset pagination.
	•	Angular 19: single rxResource/toSignal source for the list, debounceTime(300) + distinctUntilChanged() + switchMap for search, OnPush and @for (...; track order.id).
	•	Prevention: added a query-count assertion in integration tests, a k6 load test in the pipeline, and a Query Store regression alert.
	•	Result: p95 dropped from 12 s to ~300 ms (better than before the release), timeouts disappeared, and we wrote a short blameless postmortem and added the checks to our PR checklist.

Alternative example (API timeouts after a release):

	•	Situation: After a Friday release, our order-search API p95 went from 400 ms to 12 s, and users in checkout saw timeouts. Alerts from App Insights fired.
	•	Task: I was on call and responsible for restoring service and finding the root cause.
	•	Action:
	1.	Mitigate first: rolled the release back to a canary-free state and scaled out; service recovered within 20 minutes. (Stop the bleeding before investigating.)
	2.	Investigate: compared metrics before/after (CPU normal, DB DTU at 100%, blocking chains). Distributed traces showed one query dominating. Pulled the actual execution plan from Query Store: a plan regression with a clustered index scan and key lookups.
	3.	Root cause: a new filter added WHERE YEAR(CreatedAt) = @year (non-SARGable) plus an EF Include that made N+1 calls; parameter sniffing made it worse for large tenants.
	4.	Fix: rewrote the predicate as a range (CreatedAt >= @from AND < @to), added a covering index, replaced Include with a projection, and updated statistics.
	5.	Prevent: added a load test and a query-count assertion to CI, a Query Store regression alert, and a postmortem with action items.
	•	Result: p95 dropped to 250 ms (better than before), no recurrence, and the checklist was adopted by the team.

What interviewers look for: calm incident handling (mitigate → diagnose → fix → prevent), data-driven diagnosis (logs, traces, plans, dumps), ownership, communication to stakeholders, and learning.

